32958984375, -0.01995849609375, 0.0089569091796875, -0.00867462158203125, -0.003208160400390625, 0.004581451416015625, 0.006481170654296875, 0.01253509521484375, 0.006069183349609375, -0.0099029541015625, -0.0035610198974609375, 0.02935791015625, 0.0103607177734375, 0.005199432373046875, -0.02239990234375, -0.00601959228515625, -0.004375457763671875, 0.01305389404296875, -0.01806640625, 0.0108795166015625, -0.0014181137084960938, -0.0248565673828125, 0.0181427001953125, 0.0104217529296875, 0.01238250732421875, -0.01099395751953125, 0.021026611328125, 0.024169921875, 0.01009368896484375, -0.01236724853515625, -0.00717926025390625, 0.025787353515625, 0.00908660888671875, 0.006862640380859375, 0.0042724609375, -0.0174560546875, 0.00366973876953125, -0.0156402587890625, -0.01702880859375, -0.0011692047119140625, 0.001575469970703125, 0.02215576171875, -0.0577392578125, 0.02020263671875, -0.0238800048828125, -0.032379150390625, 0.0177459716796875, -0.033233642578125, 0.004932403564453125, -0.00701904296875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods have been critically overlooked. This work systematically investigates the robustness and fragility of such agents, focusing on two representative methods, and provides actionable recommendations for future research. Below, we outline the organization and key findings of the paper:\n\n- We begin by motivating the need for reliability analysis in memory-based self-improving agents and clearly state our research questions and hypotheses.\n- We then provide background on the two main paradigms of memory-based reinforcement learning, justifying our focus on EMR and MAPO.\n- Next, we describe the experimental environments, highlighting their challenges and real-world relevance.\n- The methods section is divided into subsections detailing agent architectures, training procedures, hyperparameter selection, and statistical analysis.\n- We present a manual memory analysis with a reproducible rubric and quantitative measures of annotation reliability.\n- Finally, we discuss our findings, offer recommendations, and conclude with implications for future work.\n\n**Research Questions and Contributions**\n\nThis work is guided by the following research questions, each accompanied by an explicit hypothesis or anticipated finding to clarify the study\u2019s aims:\n\n1. **RQ1: How robust are representative memory-based self-improving agent methods to stochasticity and task order?**  \n   *Hypothesis:* We anticipate that both EMR and MAPO will exhibit significant variance in performance across different random seeds and task orderings, revealing sensitivity to stochasticity and curriculum.\n\n2. **RQ2: What are the sources of fragility in agent memory banks, and how do they impact performance?**  \n   *Hypothesis:* We expect that incomplete, irrelevant, or inconsistently updated memory entries will correlate with lower agent performance, highlighting specific weaknesses in memory construction and retrieval.\n\n3. **RQ3: How does task and environment specification affect agent reliability?**  \n   *Hypothesis:* We hypothesize that providing more explicit task and environment specifications will reduce agent fragility and improve reliability, as measured by more consistent success rates and memory quality.\n\n4. **RQ4: What evaluation protocols and system design recommendations can mitigate observed fragility?**  \n   *Anticipated Finding:* We expect that adopting multi-seed evaluations, randomized task orders, and more rigorous memory analysis will reveal previously unreported weaknesses and inform best practices for future work.\n\nEach research question is directly mapped to a corresponding contribution and is discussed in the relevant results and discussion sections (see Results: RQ1\u2014Section \"Variance and Task Order Sensitivity\"; RQ2\u2014Section \"Manual Memory Analysis\"; RQ3\u2014Section \"Ablation Study on Task and Environment Specification\"; RQ4\u2014Section \"Recommendations for Evaluation and Design\").\n\nTo address these questions, our main contributions are:  \n(1) a systematic empirical analysis of the robustness of two representative memory-based self-improving agent methods (EMR and MAPO) to stochasticity and task order (addressing RQ1, see Results, Table 1 and Figure 2),  \n(2) a detailed manual analysis of agent memory banks using a reproducible rubric and quantitative agreement measures (addressing RQ2, see Results, Table 3),  \n(3) an investigation of the impact of task and environment specification on agent reliability (addressing RQ3, see Results, Table 2), and  \n(4) concrete recommendations for evaluation protocols and system design to mitigate observed fragility (addressing RQ4, see Discussion).  \n\nUnlike prior work, which typically reports single-run results on fixed task orders, we explicitly quantify variance, task order sensitivity, and the effects of underspecification, providing new insights into the reliability of these agents.\n\n**Background and Related Work**\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Replay (EMR), where agents store and retrieve past experiences to inform future actions, and (b) Memory-Augmented Policy Optimization (MAPO), where agents maintain a memory of successful trajectories to guide learning. These methods have been widely adopted in recent self-improving agent literature (e.g., Pritzel et al., 2017; Guu et al., 2017; Sun et al., 2018), and are representative of the two main paradigms in memory-based reinforcement learning: experience replay and memory-augmented policy optimization. We focus on EMR and MAPO due to their strong empirical performance, prevalence in prior work, and their contrasting approaches to memory construction and usage.\n\nOther approaches, such as meta-learning (Finn et al., 2017) and continual learning with regularization (Kirkpatrick et al., 2017), do not explicitly maintain a textual or trajectory memory bank and thus fall outside our scope. Hybrid or alternative memory-based methods (e.g., Differentiable Neural Computers (DNCs), memory networks) were considered but excluded from the main analysis for several reasons: (1) these architectures often require substantially different training regimes and hyperparameter tuning, making direct comparison challenging; (2) their use of memory is typically more implicit or less interpretable than the explicit, textual/trajectory-based banks in EMR and MAPO; and (3) prior work (e.g., Graves et al., 2016; Sukhbaatar et al., 2015) has shown that while such methods can be powerful, they are less commonly adopted in the context of continual, task-stream learning with explicit memory inspection. We provide a brief comparison to these methods in the supplementary material and argue that our findings on EMR and MAPO generalize to the most widely used and conceptually distinct paradigms in memory-based RL.\n\n**Experimental Environments**\n\nTo facilitate a clear comparison, Table 1 summarizes the main features and distinctions between the two experimental environments:\n\n| Environment                        | Task Structure                | Key Variations                | Stochasticity         | Real-world Analogy           |\n|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------|-------------------------------|-----------------------|------------------------------|\n| Multi-step Navigation Task          | Grid world navigation         | Obstacle density, path length, distractor items | Randomized obstacles and items | Robotic navigation, logistics |\n| Compositional Instruction-Following | Natural language instructions | Subgoal count, object types, action sequences, distractors | Ambiguous cues, distractor objects | Language-conditioned robotics  |\n\n- **Multi-step Navigation Task:** This environment consists of a 10x10 grid world where agents must navigate from a start to a goal location. Each of the 20 tasks varies in obstacle density, path length, and the presence of distractor items. Obstacles and items are randomized in each episode, requiring agents to generalize across layouts. The environment is designed to mimic real-world robotic navigation and logistics scenarios, where agents must adapt to dynamic obstacles and incomplete information (see, e.g., Savva et al., 2019).\n- **Compositional Instruction-Following Benchmark:** Here, agents receive natural language instructions composed of 2\u20135 subgoals, involving different object types and required actions, with distractors and ambiguous cues present. Each of the 20 tasks tests the agent\u2019s ability to parse and execute compositional instructions, similar to language-conditioned robotics or household assistants (e.g., Anderson et al., 2018). The presence of ambiguous cues and distractors challenges the agent\u2019s memory retrieval and generalization capabilities.\n\nThese environments were selected for their diversity (spanning navigation and language understanding), complexity (requiring multi-step reasoning and adaptation), and relevance to real-world applications, as supported by prior benchmarks in embodied AI and instruction following (Anderson et al., 2018; Savva et al., 2019).\n\n**Methods**\n\nTo improve clarity, we divide the methods into the following subsections:\n\n***Agent Architectures***\n\n- **EMR:** Implemented as a recurrent neural network (GRU, 2 layers, 128 hidden units) with a memory buffer of 500 entries. The agent stores episodic experiences as textual summaries and retrieves relevant entries during decision-making.\n- **MAPO:** Uses a policy network (2-layer MLP, 128 units per layer) with a memory of the top-100 successful trajectories. The memory is updated with new successful episodes and used to bias policy optimization.\n\n***Training Procedure***\n\nAgents are trained for 10,000 episodes per environment, with early stopping if the moving average success rate over 100 episodes exceeds 95%. Optimization uses Adam (learning rate 1e-3, batch size 32). Each episode presents a new task instance, with randomized environment features as described above.\n\n***Hyperparameter Selection***\n\nHyperparameters are selected via grid search on held-out tasks: for each agent and environment, we sweep learning rates {1e-4, 5e-4, 1e-3, 5e-3}, batch sizes {16, 32, 64}, and memory sizes {100, 250, 500} (for EMR) or {20, 50, 100} (for MAPO), selecting the configuration with the highest mean success rate on the validation set. Full implementation details, including code, random seeds, and environment generation scripts, are provided in the supplementary material. Supplementary Table 1 summarizes all hyperparameters and environment configurations.\n\n***Evaluation Protocols***\n\nTo comprehensively re-evaluate these methods, we broaden the scope of evaluation along two axes:  \n1. **Multiple runs:** 10 independent seeds per configuration to quantify variance. The choice of 10 seeds balances computational cost with statistical power, ensuring reliable estimation of variance and confidence intervals.  \n2. **Randomized task order:** For each run, a new random permutation of the 20 tasks is generated, ensuring each agent experiences a unique curriculum. This tests sensitivity to curriculum effects, which is critical for assessing real-world robustness.\n\nFor each agent-method pair, we report mean and standard deviation of success rates across runs and task orderings, as well as"}}